  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nMoist, southerly flow from the Gulf, thanks to a surface high \npressure center in the Midwest and a developing low pressure center \nin western South Dakota, has contributed to a low-level stratus \nlayer stretching through much of the Plains. This cloud cover will \nlimit the diurnal heating this afternoon, keeping highs in the low \nto mid 50s. Furthermore, the pressure gradient across the region \nwill allow breezy southerly winds to continue through today, with \ngusts around 35-40 MPH amidst sustained winds around 20-25 MPH. \n\nThese breezy, cloudy conditions will stick around into Wednesday, as \nthe low-pressure center starts to move northeast. Temperatures \nWednesday morning will be pretty similar to this morning, with lows \nin the mid 40s, as the cloud cover is not expected to clear out till \nmid morning/early afternoon from west to east with the upper level \nsupport. These clearing skies will allow temperatures to increase \ninto the 60s tomorrow, though lingering cloud cover may make these \ntemperatures more difficult to reach. The surface winds will follow \na similar suit, as slightly weaker westerly winds poke their head \ninto the area late morning and spread eastward through the \nafternoon. Peak wind gusts Wednesday are expected to occur mid-\nmorning, with gusts up to 35-40 MPH possible. See separate Fire\nWeather section below for more details regarding fire weather\nconcerns Wednesday in a few far northern/west-central counties.\n\nThe strong surface winds return Friday afternoon, as another \nshortwave approaches the area and spawns a low pressure center in \nthe lee of the Rockies. Southerly wind gusts of 40-45 MPH amidst \nsustained winds around 20-25 MPH will be possible once again ahead \nof an approaching cold front. Precipitation chances return to the \nforecast Friday evening through Saturday with this system, with best \nrain chances Friday evening along and north of Interstate 80. By \nSaturday, these chances primarily rest along and east of Highway 183 \nSaturday. However, model guidance has continued to trend down on the \nprecipitation chances with this system, as the shortwave and low \npressure center now track north of the area. \n\nMore seasonal temperatures will return this weekend post cold front \npassage, with a period of dry conditions forecast Sunday. However, \nprecipitation chances reenter the forecast for the start of next \nwork week.",
